<Project>

  <!--
    Version and identity for every managed project in the tree, set in one place.

    This is not cosmetic. Both shipped executables are unsigned and are downloaded from the
    internet, so the first thing a careful administrator does is right-click, Properties,
    Details. Left to the SDK defaults that tab reads:

        File description   ScanBridge.Client
        Company            ScanBridge.Client
        Product            ScanBridge.Client

    A company field naming an assembly looks like something a script assembled rather than
    something a person published. The same fields appear in Task Manager, in the UAC prompt
    the server installer raises, and in most antivirus consoles.

    (Note for anyone editing this comment: XML forbids a double hyphen inside a comment, so
    command-line switches cannot be written here in their real form. The first draft said
    "the UAC prompt raised by ScanBridge-Server.exe" followed by the install switch, and
    every project in the tree failed to load with MSB4024.)

    InformationalVersion is left to the SDK, which appends "+<commit sha>" from the git
    checkout. That is worth keeping: a released binary states in its own properties which
    commit produced it, so a bug report carrying a screenshot of that tab is traceable to a
    tree without asking the reporter anything.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <Version>1.1.0</Version>
    <Product>ScanBridge</Product>
    <Company>Dhyan Vohera</Company>
    <Authors>Dhyan Vohera</Authors>
    <Copyright>Copyright 2026 Dhyan Vohera</Copyright>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
